            <div xml:id="d245">

                <pb facs="0322" n="206" xml:id="pg206"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 839.00/1058</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to Minister Sullivan.</hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Department of State</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1914-02-12">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">February 12, 1914</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: In reply to the last paragraph of your
                    cable of February 5, noon, in reference to a United States war vessel being
                    stationed off the north coast of Santo Domingo until after the election, the
                    Department informs you that it is not deemed expedient that a vessel be now sent
                    for duty at any one point on the north coast of that republic.</p>
                <p>The U. S. S. <hi rendition="#italic">Wheeling</hi> will be stationed at Cape
                    Haitien for some weeks, and may be ordered to proceed to Puerto Plata should the
                    situation there demand her presence.</p>
                <p>I am [etc.]</p>
                <closer>For the Secretary of State: <lb/>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">J. B. Moore</hi></signed>.</closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
